          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Bangkok has a lot of strong international schools. No single one is the best. The right choice depends on the curriculum you want, your budget, where you'll live (traffic in Bangkok is a real factor) and your child's needs. These are the schools families most often shortlist:\n\n## Top-tier schools (the most established and academically strongest)\n\n**British curriculum**\n- **Shrewsbury International School**: Teaches the English National Curriculum through to IGCSE and A Levels. It has very strong exam results and campuses on the riverside and in City Campus (Watthana).\n- **Harrow International School Bangkok**: The English curriculum with A Levels, plus boarding. It's in Don Mueang, in the north of the city.\n- **Dulwich College Bangkok**: A newer school (opened in 2023) with a prestigious global brand. It teaches IGCSE and A Levels.\n- **Bangkok Patana School**: The oldest British international school in Thailand. It combines the British curriculum with the IB Diploma and has a large campus on Sukhumvit Soi 105.\n- **St Andrews International School (Sukhumvit 107 and other campuses)**: The English curriculum leading to the IB Diploma.\n\n**American curriculum and IB**\n- **International School Bangkok (ISB)**: One of the oldest and most respected schools in the city. It offers an American curriculum with AP and IB options, students of more than 50 nationalities, and big facilities. It's in Nichada Thani, Nonthaburi, which suits families living nearby.\n- **NIST International School**: A non-profit, fully IB school (PYP, MYP and DP) in central Sukhumvit, with very strong IB results.\n- **Ruamrudee International School (RIS)**: An American curriculum with AP and IB, on the eastern side of the city (Minburi).\n\n## Other well-regarded options\n- **KIS International School**: A smaller, full-IB school in Huai Khwang.\n- **Garden International School**: British curriculum, in the Sathorn area.\n- **Regent's International School**: British curriculum plus IB, with boarding.\n- **Australian International School Bangkok (AISB)**: British and Australian approaches in a smaller, community-focused setting.\n- **Wells International School** and **Bangkok Prep**: Mid-range on cost, with good reputations.\n- **Concordian International School**: Trilingual (English, Mandarin and Thai) with IB.\n- **Brighton College Bangkok** and **King's College International School Bangkok**: Newer branches of well-known UK schools.\n\n## Tips for choosing\n1. **Curriculum:** Pick British (IGCSE and A Levels), American (AP) or IB based on where your child is likely to go to university.\n2. **Location:** Commutes can take an hour or more, so many families choose where to live around the school.\n3. **Cost:** Top schools generally cost about 600,000 to more than 1,000,000 THB a year, plus one-time entrance or capital fees. Check each school's current fees.\n4. **Accreditation:** Look for CIS, WASC, BSO or IB authorization.\n5. **Waitlists:** The most popular schools, especially Shrewsbury, Patana and ISB, can have waitlists for some year groups, so apply early.\n6. **Visit:** Tours and talking to current parents, for example in expat Facebook groups, are usually the most useful way to judge a school.\n\nFees, rankings and new campuses change often, so check the details directly with each school. If you tell me your child's age, the curriculum you prefer, your budget or where you'll live, I can narrow the list down."
